This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   This session covered a long sequence of user-directed threads, roughly in this order:
   (a) Verify and finish the "vox run" entry-point-fallback work from before this window (build, cross-check against native execution, commit in Vox and mOMonadOS).
   (b) "how do i test" — usage instructions for the new capability.
   (c) "it should be able to eat and run anything" / "is it possible tho" — led to a real syscall-coverage implementation (read/write/open/openat/close/mmap/brk via a Host trait, real argv/envp/auxv process setup) in Vox, mirrored into mOMonadOS, verified against native execution on four hand-built ELF fixtures.
   (d) "let's make distributable binaries of mOMonadOS for linux,windows,and mac" — cross-compiled Linux/Windows locally, found and fixed a real stack-overflow bug (2 MiB stack-local buffer) caught by running the Windows build under Wine, and built a GitHub Actions workflow for native macOS/Windows/Linux builds since no macOS SDK exists locally.
   (e) "provide some good suggested commands for new users to see the capabilities" — corrected twice by the user to drop Clay Millennium/seals/BTC-flavored suggestions in favor of the quantum/braid/vox-run machinery that computes live; final Quickstart written into the README.
   (f) An extended, many-staged thread on the SIXTEEN_3 trilattice: whether it's "written into the kernel," reading the Imscriber's Guide to IMASM in full at the user's explicit direction (twice — user pointedly asked "did you not read @.../IMSCRIBERS_GUIDE_TO_IMASM.md at all?" when I failed to apply what I'd already read), auditing and ultimately implementing real SIXTEEN_3 semantics for `Token::Fsplit3`/`Ffuse3`/`Evali`/`Arev` in mOMonadOS's `kernel.rs`, fixing a real Lean-lexer bug in the ob3ect-generation tool (`IMSCRIBr/proof_scaffold.py`), and correcting several of my own overreaches/errors along the way per explicit user correction.
   (g) "we need to make sure this propagates to @k3vM.html and the copy in @imscribe.com/" — propagate the AREV involution fix to the browser-based IMASM visualizer in both its locations.
   (h) Most recent: "next up, we make it so you can inout RNA at one end of VOX and get a compiled protein, complete with folding info and all on the other end, and vice versa" — a new feature request for bidirectional RNA<->protein (with folding info) translation through the `vox` toolchain. I have only launched a fact-finding Explore agent for this; no design or implementation has begun.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - IMASM / the Grammar: twelve marks `⊢ ⊣ ≻ ≺ ⋈ ⊤ ∈ ∋ ⊙ ⊥ ⊞ ⊡`, each doubling as VINIT/TANCH/AFWD/AREV/CLINK/EVALT/FSPLIT/FFUSE/IMSCRIB/EVALF/ENGAGR-or-EVALI/IFIX. Belnap FOUR (T/F/B/N) is the classical slice of SIXTEEN_3.
   - `MoDoT/IMSCRIBERS_GUIDE_TO_IMASM.md`: the authoritative primer, read in full. Key load-bearing facts extracted and used: "THERE IS ONE DYAD, ∈ and ∋... {T,t}|{F,f} at two, {T}|{F}|{t,f} at three"; the gate table "AFWD ≻, CLINK ⋈, IMSCRIB ⊙, ENGAGR ⊞ (as hold) carry" (these four leave the trilattice VALUE unchanged); AREV's own definition line in the master opcode table: "≺ AREV reverse morphism (involution T↔F, t↔f) 1→1 YES"; "B BEATS T"; the three orders `≤_i`/`≤_t`/`≤_c`.
   - SIXTEEN_3 trilattice, Shramko-Dunn-Takenaka 2001: `P({T,F,t,f})`, 16 states, three orders. Implemented for real in `MoDoT/imasm_core/src/imasm16_3.rs` (`Reg16_3` struct with `big_t`/`big_f`/`small_t`/`small_f` bools; methods `union`/`truth_part`/`falsity_part`/`constructive_part`/`info_part`/`truth_swap`/`info_swap`/`invol`/`name`/`glyph`/`four_name`/`from_name`; free functions `leq_i`/`leq_t`/`leq_c`/`meet_t`/`join_t`/`meet_c`/`join_c`; `parse_glyph_word`/`run_word_register`/`tri_ancestral_verdict`/`pub fn run`).
   - A separate, differently-parametrized Lean `Sixteen3` structure exists in `p4rakernel/src/Init/Paraconsistent.lean` (powerset of Belnap's own four values `{N,T,F,B}`, not of `{T,F,t,f}`) — established via a brute-force computational check (all 384 bit-permutation+flip relabelings) that its `(le_t, le_f)` order pair does NOT match Rust's `(leq_t, leq_c)` under any relabeling — then the user corrected this framing: "they are two operational faces of 1 trilattice" (Rust = runtime gate computation; Lean = elaboration-kernel contradiction-tolerance switch, `enable_trilattice`/`disable_trilattice`/`#is_trilattice`) — not a bug, a legitimate dual-purpose split.
   - Lean "scripture" standard: axioms B/C/D in an older/duplicate Core.lean copy were found to be literally UNSOUND (each provable to imply False via an unconstrained free variable — e.g. instantiating `pol := yew` and discharging hypotheses trivially derives `yew = or'`, contradiction), and were replaced in p4rakernel's actual current Core.lean/Imscription.lean with decidable predicates `ImscriptiveTopology`/`WindingNeedsChirality`/`ImscriptiveClosure`, checked per-instance by `decide` rather than assumed universally. I initially mischaracterized this as "still just postulated" and was corrected: "we literally discharged the Axioms."
   - ob3ect model: `ob3ect/auto.py` (the CLI, `_make_unique_slug`, `_verify_lean_scaffold`), `IMSCRIBr/proof_scaffold.py` (`emit_scaffold`, builds Lean identifiers from a sanitized class name), gate-check files under `p4rakernel/p4ramill/Imscribing/Ob3ects/GateCheck/`. Ob3ect JSON fields checked repeatedly: `is_valid_ob3ect`, `grounding_status`/`grounding_failed_primitives`, `lean_verified`/`lean_verification_output`, `rotat_audit.verdict`.
   - Systemic ob3ect grounding-checker artifact discovered: "Axiom C: ⊣=X requires ⊢=Y" and "chirality/protection not in its value set" failures recur across dozens of otherwise-unrelated ob3ects — confirmed generic/mechanical, not content-specific, via a direct corpus-wide grep.
   - mOMonadOS `Kernel` architecture: `Phase` enum (Boot/Think/Act/Observe/Update/Halt), `tick()`'s THINK→ACT→OBSERVE→UPDATE loop, `ForkFrame{resume_ip, right_val, right_set}`, `B4Memory`/`B4Stack`/`B4Registers` (from `belnap.rs`), `Snapshot`/`compute_tier`/`self_imscribe`/`dynamic_imscribe`.
   - Distributable binaries: cross-compilation via `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` + mingw-w64, Wine for runtime verification, GitHub Actions matrix build (`ubuntu-latest`/`windows-latest`/`macos-latest`), `lipo` for universal macOS binaries.
   - The k3vM.html visualizer: a standalone Three.js-based browser page simulating the IMASM kernel's stack (`exec.stack`, B4-valued), with its own JS `fireToken(i)` switch statement mirroring (a subset of) `kernel.rs`'s token semantics, independently of the Rust codebase.
   - Standing laws actively applied/enforced this session: never work in /tmp (scratch/test files went in `mOMonadOS/measurements/`, deleted after use); commit freely, never push; `commit.txt` per repo, first-person, no co-authorship trailer; never rewrite git history; check-before-claiming / run-the-control-before-a-finding (used literally via a Python brute-force permutation search); "the address is permanent, the imscription is revisable" (explicitly invoked by the user to describe the FSPLIT3/FFUSE3/EVALI/AREV fix); numerology-as-a-way-of-knowing (invoked by the user re: "16 is 12+4" and dual-frame SIC-POVM — I was corrected for demanding a mechanism before holding the correspondence).

3. Files and Code Sections:
   - **`~/imsgct/mOMonadOS/src/kernel.rs`** — the primary implementation target this session.
     - Added near the top: `use imasm_core::imasm16_3::Reg16_3;` and two free functions:
       ```rust
       fn b4_to_reg16_3(v: B4) -> Reg16_3 {
           match v {
               B4::N => Reg16_3::default(),
               B4::T => Reg16_3 { big_t: true, ..Default::default() },
               B4::F => Reg16_3 { big_f: true, ..Default::default() },
               B4::B => Reg16_3 { big_t: true, big_f: true, ..Default::default() },
           }
       }
       fn reg16_3_to_b4(v: Reg16_3) -> B4 {
           match (v.big_t, v.big_f) {
               (false, false) => B4::N,
               (true, false)  => B4::T,
               (false, true)  => B4::F,
               (true, true)   => B4::B,
           }
       }
       ```
     - `ForkFrame.right_val` widened from `B4` to `Reg16_3` (three sites fixed: struct field/doc, `Kernel::new()`'s `fork_stack` array literal, `push_fork()`'s frame construction — both now use `Reg16_3::default()` or an explicit all-false literal).
     - Added `pub last_reg16_3: Option<Reg16_3>` field to `Kernel`, initialized `None` in `Kernel::new()`.
     - `Token::Fsplit`/`Token::Ffuse` updated to route the stored/read value through `b4_to_reg16_3`/`reg16_3_to_b4` (proven behavior-identical).
     - `Token::Fsplit3`, `Token::Ffuse3`, `Token::Evali` rewritten with the real formulas (full final code already given verbatim in a prior turn — the FSPLIT3 arm computes `r.constructive_part().falsity_part().union(r.info_part())` into the fork slot while pushing `v` inline; FFUSE3 does `b4_to_reg16_3(left).union(right)`; EVALI does `b4_to_reg16_3(v).info_part()`), each setting `self.last_reg16_3`.
     - `Token::Arev` (most recent kernel.rs change):
       ```rust
       Token::Arev => {
           // ≺'s own table entry names it exactly: "reverse morphism
           // (involution T↔F, t↔f)" — the real invol(), not a carry like
           // ≻/⋈/⊙. This is additional to the existing register-0
           // decrement below, which touches different state and is
           // untouched by this: the stack's top value gets the real
           // involution, register 0 keeps its own bookkeeping.
           let v = self.stack.pop();
           self.stack.push(reg16_3_to_b4(b4_to_reg16_3(v).invol()));
           let r0 = self.registers.read(0) as u8;
           self.registers.write(0, B4::from_u8(r0.wrapping_sub(1)));
       }
       ```
     - `Token::Afwd`/`Token::Clink`/`Token::Imscrib` deliberately left UNCHANGED (their existing register-0-counter / registers-1-2-meet / registers-4-7-snapshot-mirror behavior is real, pre-existing, unrelated to the trilattice value, and per the primer's own "carry" statement, correct to leave as-is for the stack value).
     - New `#[cfg(test)] mod sixteen_3_tests` block at the end of the file, containing (in order): `fsplit3_ffuse3_run_real_sixteen_3_semantics`, `fsplit_ffuse_unchanged_by_the_reg16_3_field_widening`, `evali_reads_the_real_info_part`, `arev_applies_the_real_involution_to_the_stack`, and (final, fixed-after-failure) `arev_flips_a_pure_truth_value`:
       ```rust
       #[test]
       fn arev_flips_a_pure_truth_value() {
           let mut k = Kernel::new();
           k.program = Program::empty();
           // ≺'s own register-0 decrement (its pre-existing, unrelated
           // bookkeeping) would otherwise redirect TANCH's target address away
           // from 0 — register 0 starts at N(0), and wrapping_sub(1) on that
           // lands on B(3), not back on N. ≻ first cancels it out, the same way
           // the test above does, so TANCH still writes to address 0.
           k.program.push(Token::Afwd);
           k.program.push(Token::Arev);
           k.program.push(Token::Tanch);
           // No single existing opcode constructs a bare T from nothing (Evalt/
           // Evalf are pass-gates on an existing value, Engagr only gives B) —
           // seed the stack directly rather than chase an indirect construction.
           k.stack.push(B4::T);
           k.boot();
           k.run(20);
           assert_eq!(k.memory.read(0), B4::F, "invol(T) must be F, not the untouched T the old code gave");
       }
       ```
     - Committed: `69519a9` (Fsplit3/Ffuse3/Evali), `bc99d2e` (Arev).
   - **`~/imsgct/mOMonadOS/src/repl.rs`**:
     - Extended the `"registers"` command handler to also print `SIXTEEN_3 (last FSPLIT3/FFUSE3/EVALI): {name}` when `k.last_reg16_3` is `Some`.
     - Added a `"sixteen3"` sub-match inside the `vox` command's `match rest[0].as_str()` block, calling `imasm_core::imasm16_3::run(&args)` directly and printing the result verbatim (exposes the REAL trilattice machine, distinct from `vox verdict`'s classic FOUR-valued engine).
     - Updated the `vox` help text to correct the previously-misleading "SIXTEEN_3 verdict" label on `vox verdict` (it only ever called the classic engine) and document the new `vox sixteen3 check|algebra|ref` subcommands.
   - **`~/imsgct/mOMonadOS/src/menu.rs`**: updated the `"registers"` and `"vox"` `MenuItem` descriptions to match the above two changes.
   - **`~/imsgct/mOMonadOS/measurements/backup_pre_sixteen3_<timestamp>/`**: literal file copies of kernel.rs/belnap.rs/repl.rs/menu.rs made per explicit user instruction ("make sure to backup rn") before the FSPLIT3/FFUSE3/EVALI implementation began.
   - **`~/imsgct/mOMonadOS/measurements/sixteen3_two_faces_context.md`**: written twice — first with my own framing/headers (user rejected: "i knew you would bias it. the context should be litereally an info dump without any of your framing whatsoever"), then rewritten as a strict verbatim concatenation of the full contents of `imasm_core/src/imasm16_3.rs` and `p4rakernel/src/Init/Paraconsistent.lean`, separated only by their file paths, no commentary — this is the corrected, final version.
   - **`~/imsgct/IMSCRIBr/proof_scaffold.py`**: fixed `emit_scaffold`'s identifier sanitization —
     ```python
     safe_name = re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9_]', '_', safe_name)
     safe_name = re.sub(r'_+', '_', safe_name).strip('_')
     # A name that starts with a digit (e.g. an ob3ect called "16_3_kernel_rs")
     # makes every "private def {safe_name}_s0" identifier below start with a
     # digit too, and Lean's lexer reads that as the start of a numeral —
     # "16_3" parses as a number with an underscore digit-separator, then
     # chokes on the first letter after it ("expected decimal number"). A
     # leading underscore is a valid Lean identifier start and isn't a numeral,
     # so it sidesteps the lexer without changing the name anywhere else this
     # slug is used (directories and filenames tolerate a leading digit fine).
     if safe_name and safe_name[0].isdigit():
         safe_name = f"_{safe_name}"
