The stages are linearly ordered because each operation type requires the output of the prior. Logical operations on the
bare category (Stage 1) must precede inductive extensions (Stage 2) because the logical properties determine which
inductive constructions are meaningful. Inductive extensions must precede algebraic enrichments
(Stage 3) because the
monoidal product (A1) requires the free cocompletion (I1) to define its coherence constraints. Algebraic enrichments
must precede the second logical pass (Stage 4) because L3 (cartesian closure), L5 (Frobenius), and L6 (paraconsistent
negation) all require monoidal structure (A1) to be in place.
This linear order is not chosen. It is the unique topological ordering of the dependency DAG. Any alternative ordering
would ask a question before its preconditions are available, producing a category-theoretically malformed query. The
induction path is forced by the preconditions of the operations themselves.
